1. Executive Summary & Architectural Philosophy
Robinson's Toolkit MCP v2.0 is designed as a "Fat Server, Lean Client" orchestration gateway.
It strictly adheres to the Model Context Protocol (MCP). It is a universally project-agnostic
developer utility—a highly advanced "mechanic's garage" used by an AI agent (the "Brain") to
build target projects like YardSync and Cortiware. It is NOT an Agentic OS, nor is it a runtime
dependency for the final built applications.
1.1 The "Anti-OS" Boundaries
To ensure the toolkit remains a compliant, fast, and predictable MCP server, the following
boundaries are absolute:
● No Multi-Agent Orchestration: The toolkit will NOT spawn sub-agents or worker LLMs.
All workflows are managed by deterministic Node.js handler functions.
● No Persisted Memory Layer: The toolkit will not store conversational history or vector
databases of past chats. The LLM client (e.g., Claude Desktop, Cursor) is solely
responsible for conversation memory.
● Strict Gateway Principles: All API limits, credential management (via .env), and
rate-limiting are handled synchronously by the Node.js process.

2. Phase 1: Eradicating the "Tools Tax"
The core innovation of v2.0 is the complete elimination of prompt bloat ("Agent Amnesia") by
preventing the mass injection of 1,400+ tool schemas into the LLM's context window.
2.1 Semantic Routing & Graceful Degradation
The bridge (index.js) will initialize an in-memory semantic vector matrix using the
Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 ONNX model. Furthermore, the bridge must enforce Graceful
Degradation: during bootstrap, the server will probe process.env and silently drop entire
namespaces from the semantic matrix if their respective credentials are missing. The server
must never crash due to missing keys.
// EXAMPLE ONLY - DO NOT COPY DIRECTLY
const activeNamespaces = {

stripe: !!process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY,
vercel: !!process.env.VERCEL_TOKEN,
neon: !!process.env.NEON_API_KEY
};
async function initializeSemanticRouter(registry) {
extractor = await pipeline('feature-extraction', 'Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2');
for (const tool of registry) {
const namespace = tool.name.split('_')[0];
// Graceful Degradation: Silently skip tool if .env key is missing
if (!activeNamespaces[namespace] && namespace !== 'local' && namespace !==
'compound') continue;
const text = `${tool.name}: ${tool.description}`.toLowerCase();
const output = await extractor(text, { pooling: 'mean', normalize: true });
registryMatrix.push({ ...tool, vector: output.data });
}
}

2.2 Two-Phase Lazy Schema Loading
When the LLM calls search_toolkit, it will receive only the tool names and brief descriptions. The
LLM must explicitly call a subsequent retrieval tool (e.g., get_tool_schema) to inject the heavy
JSON schema and enums into its context window just-in-time before execution.

3. Phase 2: Hardware-Level Sandboxing
Executing untrusted LLM shell commands directly via child_process.exec in local.js is a severe
security risk. The architecture will replace application-layer path gating with ephemeral hardware
isolation.
3.1 MicroVM Execution Lifecycle
Wrap local_run_command in a Firecracker microVM or gVisor sandbox. The execution lifecycle
must follow an atomic process:
1. Provision: Clone only the required WORKSPACE_ROOT directory into the VM.
2. Run: Execute the command in an isolated kernel network namespace.
3. Teardown & Sync: Capture output, validate artifacts against egress rules, sync approved

files back to the host, and instantly destroy the VM.

4. Phase 3: Reversible State & Local-Cloud Bridging
4.1 The Observability Ledger (Deterministic Rollback)
Every state-mutating tool must become an atomic transaction with a deterministic rollback path.
The router will maintain an observability ledger. When a mutation tool is called (e.g.,
github_create_branch), its inverse receipt (e.g., github_delete_branch) is pushed to the ledger.
The toolkit will expose a compound_rollback_transaction tool, allowing the agent to flush the
ledger and revert changes if a hallucination or error occurs.
4.2 Local-Cloud Bridging ("God Mode")
The AI agent is explicitly instructed to act as a bridge between cloud infrastructure and the local
filesystem. When the agent uses a cloud tool to generate resources (e.g., provisioning a Neon
database), it must autonomously use the local_write_file tool to inject the resulting credentials
(e.g., connection strings) directly into the local project's .env.local file without requiring manual
developer intervention.

5. Phase 4: The "Dev-Plugin" Architecture
(Project-Agnostic Super Tools)
To ensure the MCP server remains universally applicable, domain-specific logic (e.g., YardSync
routing) must never be hardcoded into the core MCP handlers. Instead, project-specific "Super
Tools" will be implemented as toggleable Dev-Plugins.
5.1 Environment-Gated Plugin Namespaces
Create a handlers/dev-plugins/ directory. Plugins are activated via .env flags (e.g.,
ENABLE_YARDSYNC_DEV_TOOLS=true). These tools are strictly for scaffolding, mocking,
and simulating during development, NOT for runtime production logic.
